{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>There shall not be found among you one who passes his son and his daughter through fire, a diviner of divinations, a soothsayer, an augur, and a sorcerer, or a charmer, or one who consults a familiar spirit, or a wizard, or one who inquires of the dead.</b> Scripture enumerates nine abominations in which the Canaanites engaged, and it begins with Molech, which is the most severe of them all — to offer the fruit of his body to idolatry — and because of its severity it is liable to karet (excision), unlike all the rest, which are prohibited by a negative commandment. And after He warned them concerning the nine, not to engage in them, He commanded him to hold fast to the fear of HaShem — that is what it says: ״תמים תהיה עם YHWH Elohimיך״ (you shall be wholehearted with YHWH your Elohim). And the meaning of מעביר is that he would pass him between two pyres of fire with his feet; and I have already mentioned the matter of Molech in its place." "<b>A diviner of divinations.</b> This is one who takes his staff in his hand and says, 'Shall I go to such-and-such a place or shall I not go?' — and this is what the prophet said (Hosea 4:12): ״עמי בעצו ישאל ומקלו יגיד לו״ (My people consult their wood, and their staff declares to them). <b>A soothsayer (מעונן).</b> There are in it three meanings: the sense of עונה (season/time), the sense of עין (eye), and the sense of ענן (cloud). עונה — that is how the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted it: Rabbi Akiva says: מעונן — these are those who designate times, who say, 'Such-and-such a time is propitious to begin.' And the Sages say: these are deceivers of the eyes (אוחזי העינים). And there are those who interpreted מעונן as one who gazes at the clouds. <b>An augur (מנחש).</b> A morsel fell from his mouth, a deer crossed his path. And there are those who interpreted מנחש as one who observes birds — in their wings or in their chirping — in the manner of what is written (Ecclesiastes 10:20): ״כי עוף השמים יוליך את הקול״ (for a bird of the heavens will carry the voice) — this refers to one who recognizes the chirping; and ״בעל הכנפים״ (the master of wings) — this refers to one who recognizes the flapping of the wings or their spreading." "<b>And a sorcerer.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: מכשף is a general term for all sorceries, and חובר חבר and שואל אוב וידעוני are specific categories of sorcery, and He prohibited them also from being consulted. Know and understand concerning the matter of sorceries: that when the Creator, may He be exalted, created everything from nothing, He made the supernal beings the governors of the lower beings that are beneath them; He placed the power of the earth and all that is before it in the stars and constellations, according to their governance. And He further set over the stars and constellations governors — angels and celestial officers — which are a soul to them; and behold, their governance is from the time of their coming into being until forever, according to the decree of the Most High that He established for them. But it was among His mighty wonders that He placed in the power of the supernal governors ways of exchange and forces to transform that which is beneath them — so that if the gaze of the stars, upon the face corresponding to the earth, be good or evil for the land, for a nation, or for a man, those supernal faces may transform it to its opposite by means of the gaze associated with it — in the manner that they said: תמורת ענג נגע (a blemish in place of delight). And He did this so that the Creator, may His name be exalted, may (Daniel 2:21) ״מהשנא עדניא וזמניא״ (change times and seasons), and (Amos 5:8) ״קורא למי הים״ (call the waters of the sea) to rule over them as He wills, and turn morning into darkness without changing the nature of the world, and so that the stars and constellations continue their course in their order. Therefore said the author of Sefer ha-Levanah, the sage, in nigromancy and in astrology: when the moon — which is the sphere of the world — appears, for example, at the head of Aries, and its face is in such-and-such a constellation, you should make an image for such-and-such a thing and engrave upon it the name of the hour and the name of the angel appointed over it, from among those names mentioned in that book, and perform such-and-such an incensation in such-and-such a manner, and its gaze upon it will be for evil — to uproot, to pull down, to destroy, and to overthrow. And when the moon is in such-and-such an hour and in such-and-such a constellation, make an image and an incensation in such-and-such a manner for all good — to build and to plant. Now behold, this too is the governance of the moon and the power of its governors; but the simple governance which is in its course is the will of the Creator, may He be exalted, which He placed in them from of old. And this is the secret of sorceries and their power — for they said of them that they 'contradict the celestial retinue,' meaning that they are the opposite of the simple forces, and they constitute a contradiction to the retinue in one respect from among various respects. Therefore it is fitting that the Torah prohibit them, so that the world be left to its governance and to its simple nature, which is the will of its Creator. And this is also among the reasons for the prohibition of kilayim (diverse kinds), for from the hybridizations there may come plants that produce foreign effects and generate changes from the normal course of the world, for evil or for good — besides the fact that they themselves constitute a change in creation. Thus far the words of the Rav, of blessed memory." "<b>And I saw</b> in the book of the knowledge of the stars and their spirituality, called Iggeret Galenus, that one who makes a certain image of iron for the star Saturn, in a certain hour and in a certain constellation, and buries it beneath the shop of a merchant — it will cause his profit to be lost; and if he buries it in his house, all his affairs will be harmed. And it is known to every discerning person that this wisdom is the destruction of the world, and the world's persistence and existence is only if it proceeds according to its simple nature in which the Creator, blessed be He, created and renewed it. They also mentioned there in that epistle, that one who wishes to draw down to himself the spirituality of Saturn — which is the star that serves on the day of Shabbat — must reduce his eating of meat and of all other foods, and refrain from conjugal relations, and wear black garments, and make an image of a lion of bronze, with the hour set for Saturn and the constellation Capricorn, and slaughter a black cat over that image; and then [the spirituality of Saturn] will draw near to him to do his work and will tell him future events. Thus far from that epistle; I have transcribed it from the foreign language into a clear language, namely the holy tongue. Now behold, this pertains to the wisdom of the Torah, which surpasses the wisdom of the stars and constellations, for it commanded us in the mitzvah of Shabbat, whose star is Saturn, to act in the opposite of all this. For this wisdom is a great cause of the soul's diseases, and the wisdom of the Torah is for them a bandage and a plaster. This wisdom forbade eating meat on the day of Shabbat and reducing food, but we were commanded to delight on the day of Shabbat — to eat meat and drink wine and everything that pleases us, as it is said (Isaiah 58:13): ״וקראת לשבת ענג״ (and you shall call the Sabbath a delight). This wisdom forbade conjugal relations, but we make marital relations fundamental on Shabbat, as it is said (Psalms 1:3): ״אשר פריו יתן בעתו״ (which yields its fruit in its season). This wisdom dresses one in black, but we put on מילת וצבעונין (fine and colored garments) on the day of Shabbat, as it is said (Isaiah 58:13): ״וכבדתו מעשות דרכיך״ (and honor it, not doing your ways) — and these are the honored garments; for so Rabbi Yochanan called his garments מכבדותי (my honorers). And blessed is El Elyon who granted us knowledge and discernment in knowing His Torah and His commandments, and prevented us from straying after the stars and constellations — that is what He said (Deuteronomy 4:19): ״ופן תשא עיניך השמימה וראית את השמש ואת הירח״ (lest you lift up your eyes to the heavens and see the sun and the moon) — and bequeathed us the commandments to merit through them, and so that by observing them they be for us ״עקב רב״ (a great reward)." "<b>Or a charmer.</b> This is one who gathers demons and binds them together, or one who brings together and assembles snakes, scorpions, and other creatures in one place. <b>Or one who consults a familiar spirit.</b> It is one of the categories of sorcery; its name is Pythom. It raises the dead from under its armpit and speaks from its armpit. And concerning the matter of the woman with a familiar spirit (ba'alat ov) mentioned in the account of Saul, there are divergent views among the early authorities, of blessed memory. One says: it is impossible to revive the dead through the craft of the ov, and perish the thought that that woman revived Samuel and brought him up; rather, that entire affair was all craft and deception, for she recognized him immediately that he was Saul and did not wish to reveal it to him until afterward, when she said to him (1 Samuel 28:12): ״ולמה רמיתני ואתה שאול״ (Why have you deceived me, and you are Saul?) — by her words she showed him that she had now discovered the matter through her craft. And the way of every ba'alat ov is to hide a person, and he speaks softly from within the hiding place, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 29:4): ״והיה כאוב מארץ קולך״ (and your voice shall be like a ghost from the earth). And so this ba'alat ov hid a man and told Saul that he was Samuel; and what is written (1 Samuel 28:7): ״ויאמר שאול אל שמואל״ (And Saul said to Samuel) — this is said with respect to his thought, for he thought that it was Samuel. And what he said (ibid.): ״ולא עשית חרון אפו בעמלק״ (and you did not execute the fierceness of His wrath upon Amalek) — this was well known throughout the land, that Samuel the prophet had said so during his lifetime. And what he said (ibid.): ״מחר אתה ובניך עמי״ (Tomorrow you and your sons will be with me) — he said this as a matter of reasoning, for she had so instructed that hidden speaker, so that Saul would think him to be Samuel; for she knew the prophet's pronouncement concerning Saul's punishment in the matter of Amalek, and now that the Philistines were waging war against him, she reckoned that he would fall into their hands." "<b>There are those</b> who say that it was Samuel in very truth, and he told him all the matters mentioned in the passage; and so is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory. And one may raise a difficulty on this: since it was Samuel in very truth, how is it possible for a person to revive the dead through the art of the ov? But the opinion of the Geonim, Rav Hai and Rabbeinu Saadyah, is that the woman did not revive him, but the Holy One, blessed be He, revived him. And this is their language: 'It is far-fetched that the woman should know the future, and likewise that she should revive the dead through the art of the ov; rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, revived Samuel in order to tell Saul all the things that were destined to come upon him, and the woman knew nothing of all this at all; therefore she was alarmed and cried out, as it is written (I Samuel 28:12): ״ותזעק בקול גדול ותאמר אלהים ראיתי עולים מן הארץ״; and what she said (ibid.) ״את מי אעלה לך״ — such was their custom to say, and it was words of pretense' — thus far." "<b>And behold,</b> the Scriptures concur with the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory and the opinion of these Geonim, and with reason, that it was Samuel in very truth, who was revived and spoke; but the woman did not revive him, for it is not in the hand of man to revive the dead, but only in the hand of the Creator, blessed be He, and through the prophets; and so is the opinion of the sage Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, of blessed memory." "<b>There is</b> yet another opinion among some of our masters, the sages of the Talmud, of blessed memory: that there is power in one who knows the art of the ov to revive the dead and restore his soul into his body, and this is only within twelve months; and this is what they said: a certain heretic said to Rabbi So-and-so: 'You say that the souls of the righteous are stored beneath the Throne of Glory — how did an ov raise Samuel in the necropolis?' He said to him: 'For it was taught: for twelve months his body persists and his soul ascends and descends; after twelve months his body is nullified and his soul ascends and no longer descends.' We learn from this that there is power in the hand of one who knows this art to restore his soul into his body within twelve months, but not after twelve months, for the body is nullified and the soul ascends, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 12:7): ״ושב העפר על הארץ כשהיה והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה״ — when the dust returns to the earth, then the spirit returns to the Elohim who gave it. And know that what it says, 'his body persists' and 'is nullified' — the matter does not depend on the persistence of the body or its nullification, for even if it decayed within three days and is nullified, his soul still ascends and descends; and likewise if it was embalmed with substances that preserve it for many years, his soul after twelve months immediately ascends and no longer descends. If so, the meaning of 'his body persists' and 'is nullified' is relative to the soul, which was accustomed to it and made it a vessel." "<b>And a yidde'oni.</b> This is the craft of one who places the bone of a living creature whose name is ידוע (yado'a) inside his mouth and speaks by means of sorcery; and that creature is called by the people of the field בני השדה, and this is what is written (Job 5:23–24): ״כי עם אבני השדה בריתך וחית השדה השלמה לך״ — for the alef is superfluous. <b>Or one who inquires of the dead.</b> This is one who raises by means of a skull, and the inquiring is by means of a skull (גלגלת), or in any other manner that he may devise for them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {4 "The author cites Ramban at length. The closing of comment [2] is marked עכ״ל הרב ז״ל ('thus far the words of the Rav, of blessed memory'), clearly delimiting the citation. Comment [3] opens with וראיתי ('And I saw'), returning to Rabbeinu Bahya's own voice. The two comments were supplied as separate entries and are rendered separately."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:05.085643593Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}